sentences.l:
From all of these, treasures many and good
I took out, and everything bringing to Agamemnon I used to give,
Atreus’-son;

Truly indeed he has toiled very much without me,
and has built a wall, and driven a ditch close by it,
wide and great, and in it he has fixed stakes;

but while I was fighting among the Achaeans
Hector was not willing to rouse battle away from the wall,
but only as far as the Scaean Gates and the oak he came;

You will see, if you wish, and if these things concern you,
very early upon the fish-teeming Hellespont sailing
my ships, and in them men eager to row;

and other gold from here, and red bronze,
and well-girdled women, and grey iron
I shall take, such as I got by lot;

but my prize, the very one who gave it,
again in insolence took, lord Agamemnon,
Atreus’-son;

to him everything declare, as I enjoin,
openly, so that the other Achaeans too may be angry,
if any one of the Danaans he still hopes to deceive,
always in shamelessness clothed;

The daughter of Agamemnon, Atreus’-son, I will not marry,
not even if in beauty she should rival golden Aphrodite,
and in works match gleaming-eyed Athena;

There to me very much has urged my manly spirit,
having married a wedded wife, a fitting wife,
to take pleasure in the possessions that the old man Peleus acquired;

And indeed to the others I would give counsel
to sail away home, since no longer will you find an end
of steep Ilios;

And Phoenix here with us remaining let him lie down to sleep,
so that with me in the ships to my dear fatherland he may follow
tomorrow, if he wishes;
